ity of California, Berkeley, who was one of the founders of modern Andean studies.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-hunyadi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Hunyadi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Hunyadi was a Hungarian general and nobleman of Wallachian origin who led the fight against the Ottomans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-hyrcanus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Hyrcanus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Hyrcanus was Hasmonean high priest and ruler of Judea from 134 to 104 BCE, son of Simon Thassi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-albert.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I Albert</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I Albert was a Jagiellonian king of Poland from 1492 to 1501, son of Casimir IV.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-doukas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-doukas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I Doukas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I Doukas was ruler of Thessaly from about 1268 until his death, an illegitimate son of Michael II of Epirus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-of-castile.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I of Castile</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I was king of Castile from 1379 to 1390, the son of Henry II and second king of the House of Trastámara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-gaeta"><span class="enc-name">John I of Gaeta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I was hypatus, or ruler, of Gaeta in the early 10th century, succeeding his father Docibilis I, founder of the dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-of-portugal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I of Portugal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I of Portugal was the founder of the House of Aviz, who secured the throne after a succession crisis in the late fourteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-sweden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-of-sweden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I of Sweden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I of Sweden, John Sverkersson, was king of Sweden from 1216 to 1222, the last of the House of Sverker.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-tzimiskes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I Tzimiskes</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I Tzimiskes was Byzantine emperor from 969 to 976, an Armenian-born general who seized the throne by murdering his uncle Nikephoros II Phokas.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-count-of-nassau-weilburg"><span class="enc-name">John I, Count of Nassau-Weilburg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I was count of Nassau-Weilburg from the partition of 1344, ruling lands of the Walramian line of the house of Nassau south of the Lahn.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-brabant"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-i-duke-of-brabant.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John I, Duke of Brabant</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I, Duke of Brabant, ruled from 1267 to 1294 and was celebrated as a knightly hero and poet.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-mecklenburg-stargard"><span class="enc-name">John I, Duke of Mecklenburg-Stargard</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John I was a son of Henry II of Mecklenburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-doukas"><span class="enc-name">John II Doukas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John II Doukas was ruler of Thessaly from 1303 to 1318, a grandson of John I Doukas, who had made Thessaly a separate principality.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-komnenos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-ii-komnenos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John II Komnenos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John II Komnenos was Byzantine emperor from 1118 to 1143, the son of Alexios I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-aragon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John II of Aragon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John II was King of Navarre by marriage from 1425 and King of Aragon from 1458 until his death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/ency