span class="enc-snip">John of Luxembourg, known as John the Blind, was King of Bohemia from 1310 to 1346.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-capistrano"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-of-capistrano.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John of Capistrano</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Capistrano was an Italian Franciscan friar and preacher who campaigned across central Europe against the Hussites and for a crusade against the Ottomans.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gaunt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-of-gaunt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John of Gaunt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, was the third surviving son of Edward III of England and Philippa of Hainaut, born at Ghent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gischala"><span class="enc-name">John of Gischala</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Gischala was a leader of the First Jewish–Roman War from the town of Gischala in Galilee.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-count-of-jaffa"><span class="enc-name">John of Ibelin, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Ibelin, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon, was a nephew of the Old Lord of Beirut and the leading jurist of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-lord-of-arsuf"><span class="enc-name">John of Ibelin, Lord of Arsuf</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Ibelin, Lord of Arsuf, was a son of John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-the-old-lord-of-beirut"><span class="enc-name">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut, was a half-brother of Queen Isabella I of Jerusalem and the most powerful baron of the crusader states in his time.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay-earl-of-ross"><span class="enc-name">John of Islay, Earl of Ross</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Islay, also called John MacDonald II, was the last Lord of the Isles and Earl of Ross.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-islay"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-of-islay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John of Islay, Lord of the Isles</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Islay was a chief of Clan Donald, descended from Somerled, who was the first to style himself Lord of the Isles (Dominus Insularum).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-leiden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-of-leiden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John of Leiden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Leiden, born Jan Beukelszoon, was a Dutch Anabaptist leader, a tailor from Leiden, who ruled Münster during the Anabaptist rebellion.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-montfort"><span class="enc-name">John of Montfort</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Montfort was lord of Tyre in the crusader Kingdom of Jerusalem from 1270, succeeding his father Philip of Montfort, who was murdered by an Assassin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-neuilly"><span class="enc-name">John of Neuilly</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Neuilly was a Frankish knight and hereditary marshal of the Principality of Achaea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-saxony"><span class="enc-name">John of Saxony</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Saxony was king of Saxony from 1854 to 1873.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-scotland-earl-of-huntingdon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-of-scotland-earl-of-huntingdon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John of Scotland, Earl of Huntingdon</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of Scotland, known as John the Scot, was Earl of Huntingdon and, from 1232, Earl of Chester.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-of-the-cross.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John of the Cross</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John of the Cross was a Spanish Carmelite friar, priest and mystic who worked with Teresa of Ávila to reform the Carmelite order.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-okello"><span class="enc-name">John Okello</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Okello was a Ugandan-born labourer and revolutionary who led the armed rising of 12 January 1964 that overthrew the Sultan of Zanzibar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-papa-i-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-papa-i-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Papa ʻĪʻī</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Papa ʻĪʻī was a Hawaiian politician and historian, born on Oʻahu in 1800 into a family of hereditary kahu, caretakers of the chiefs.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-pascoe-fawkner"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-pascoe-fawkner.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">John Pascoe Fawkner</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">John Pascoe Fawkner was an Australian pioneer, businessman and politician who helped found Melbourne in 1835.</span></a></li></ul>
